            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has some air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Majority of air leakage is happening through the crawl space ceiling making your main floor cold and uncomfortable. During my investigation I noticed some exposed insulation on the ceiling joists. I would recommend sealing it perfectly with the vapour barrier.

** Ceiling on the second floor: During our investigation with the IR camera, we noticed some cold spots on the ceiling which could be air leakage. Please keep an eye on these spots to see if there is any frost build up in winter times. It looks like there is no ventilation in the attic and there is a possibility of moisture buildup in the attic resulting in moist insulation.   

** The outside doors - these doors are not sealing properly (can seal with weatherstripping)

** Some of the electrical outlets in exterior walls of the house - e.g. the one near the entrance door. Foam wall plate gaskets can be installed behind the plates..



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 280 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 7.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.





</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 with R12 batt insulation and 1.5 inches(R7.5) rigid foam extruded polystrene insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam polystyrene insulation to these exterior walls. 



Savings: By adding 3 inches(R15) rigid foam extruded polystrene insulation to these walls, you could potentially save $820 in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is strongly recommended and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval recommendation. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

https://aea.nt.ca/program/energy-efficient-products/

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls appear to be 2x6 framing with R20 insulation. I have modelled adding 3????????? (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls.



Savings: By adding 3????????? (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls, you could potentially save $ 80 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows />
                <doors>

One of the exterior door in this house is older solid wood door with double pane glass windows and you are planning to replace this door. I recommend you upgrade this older door to an ENERGY STAR?????? certified doors.



Rebates: By replacing this older door with one that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 10 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 0.3 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 151 litre gas-fired hot water tank. You may want to consider replacing your hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, could potentially save $ 100 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 82.2% efficient gas-fired boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing gas-fired boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE.



Savings: By replacing your current gas-fired boiler with a 95% AFUE condensing gas-fired boiler, you could potentially save $ 1,800 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 46.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). 



While updating your boiler, you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for up to $250 in rebates per pump (up to 2 per fiscal year) from EEIP.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



Savings: By installing a 5 kW solar photovoltaic system on the front facing roof of your house (orientation = S), you could potentially save $ 1,500 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 20,000 from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount is based on the lower of:

1. 50% of the eligible cost of the system, up to a maximum of $20,000.

2. A calculation of $3.50 per watt that the system can produce.



Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program









</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights ????????? Non-hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulb and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.9 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current gas-fired boiler and hot water tank co-vent up a chimney and is susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present on the main floor but not in the basement. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house: 

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Inuvik



The following Dec. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:



Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.73 per liter 

Nat gas - $37.21 per GJ

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $14.70 per bag, $682.50 per tonne bulk delivered

